# Project Context
You are working in a beginner-friendly HCDE coursework project.

# Project Context Profile
- Practitioner background: HCD practitioner (not a software engineer), working in UX research/UX design contexts.
- Project: Mini Project 2 — Type System Builder. A browser-based tool that helps users find, pair, and preview fonts, then export a complete type system for use in brand or design work.
- Primary goal: Let users build a type system (typeface, weight, size scale, use case) within ~30 minutes, without feeling overwhelmed.
- Primary audience: Anyone working on a design project — designers, students, and people working on personal brands. Users may have no design or coding background.
- Coding style preference: Beginner-friendly explanations and code style.
- Implementation preference: Balance clarity and compactness.
- Error-handling preference: Fail fast with clear, actionable error messages.
- Dependency preference: Default to dependency-free solutions. No npm, no build tools, no frameworks unless explicitly requested.

# Tech Stack
- Single standalone HTML file with embedded CSS and vanilla JavaScript.
- Google Fonts API for fetching font data and loading font previews via embed links.
- No backend, no login, no server required — runs by opening the HTML file in a browser.
- API key (if required) stored in a `.env` file and never committed to version control.

# UI and UX Conventions
- Present one decision at a time to avoid decision paralysis. Do not show all options at once.
- Use clear labels, headings, and helper text so users always know what step they are on.
- Filters (typeface category, mood, use case, weight) should narrow options progressively.
- Font pairing suggestions appear after a primary font is selected — not before.
- A "Preview this pairing" button renders the live preview; do not auto-preview on every filter change.
- Preview sample text is always consistent: a title (brand name), a heading (short tagline), and body copy (1–2 sentences). Never use random or lorem ipsum text.
- The exported type system includes: primary typeface, paired typeface, weights, size scale, and use case labels (display, heading, body, caption).

# Font Preview
- Load fonts using Google Fonts embed URLs (e.g. `https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=...`).
- Apply fonts via inline `style` attributes or dynamically injected `<link>` tags — no separate CSS files.
- Preview renders in the browser using the actual typefaces, not images.

# Directory Structure
Keep this section and the directory tree current whenever files are added, removed, or renamed.

HCDE 530 Week 8 — Mini Project 2/
- `.cursorrules` (Cursor AI guidance and rules)
- `mp2_project_declaration.md` (project declaration)
- `index.html` (standalone Type System Builder app)

# Project Conventions
- Prefer simple, readable code over clever one-liners.
- Explain changes in plain language suitable for someone learning to code.
- Keep scripts self-contained and easy to run locally (open HTML file in browser).
- Prioritize beginner readability while avoiding unnecessary verbosity.
- Keep defaults aligned to the Project Context Profile unless the user asks to override.

# For HTML/CSS/JS Files
- Use clear variable and function names (`selectedFont`, `pairingList`, `renderPreview`, etc.).
- Add short comments for non-obvious logic only — do not narrate what every line does.
- Avoid adding external libraries unless explicitly requested.
- Preserve existing behavior unless the request asks for functional changes.
- Default UI to high-level, step-by-step flow; add advanced options only on request.

# For API Work
- Validate that the API call succeeds before rendering results; show a clear error if it fails.
- Do not silently ignore empty or malformed API responses.
- Keep API key out of the HTML file; load from `.env` if needed and note in comments.

# When Unsure
- Ask one concise clarifying question before large structural changes.
- If project files are added, removed, or renamed, update the # Directory Structure section above.
